    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Stonehenge was built and rebuilt over some 1,500 years, from an earthwork of about 3100 BC to the sarsen circle of 2600 to 2400 BC and later rearrangements of the bluestones.</li><li>It was a place of burial from its beginnings, and its builders moved bluestones some 140 to 150 miles from the Preseli Hills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</li><li>Its purpose remains debated: an observatory, a place of healing, a domain of the dead linked to Durrington Walls, or a symbol of unity.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Cecil Chubb bought Stonehenge at auction in 1915 for £6,600, reportedly on a whim because he believed a local man should own it, and gave it to the nation three years later.</p></aside>
    </section>

